>From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)

>What follows are some personal comments about this list, about what
>may or may not be broken or damaged.

[Rest elided]

Tim;

Don't take this personally, but you are taking the list too
personally. I know lots of other people think its broken, but it ISN'T
broken. What is broken is that many people take the list too
personally.

This list is not especially high volume compared to some, the postings
are of fairly high quality, and there is a very, almost
extraordinarily, low level of flaming. The only problem is that
certain list users spend too much time reading the list and project
too much of their egos into it. If everyone would just feel free to
ignore messages or delete them if they felt like it, and if people
would stop taking it seriously, they would realize there is really no
problem.

I've been using mailing lists for over a decade. I always will
remember the first big list meltdown I ever took part in -- the
destruction of the SF-Writers mailing list run by Chuq von Rospach,
which I was a part of. Lots of people started taking the list way too
seriously, lots of flames happened, and for stupid reasons lots of
people got their feelings hurt. Later on, when Chuq resigned as list
moderator in a huff, I took over the list management. That was my
first experience running an internet mailing list. What I've learned
since then is this: lists operate best by benign neglect. The more
rules, the more attempts to restructure, the more meta-discussion
about whats wrong with the list (and this sort of discussion occurs on
EVERY mailing list, period), the worse things get.

The extropians mailing list literally has nothing wrong with it, other
than a slightly excessive volume. You can deal with the excess volume
by reading the digest instead of the reflector. We even seem to have
hit on a great way to stop flame wars, which is our PPL mechanism.
When people say they are perceiving problems, what they are really
likely noting is that they spend way too much time reading the list
way too carefully, and as a result they are getting upset by the usual
sorts of foolishness that occur in all human interactions. Magnified
by the ability to read and reread these stupidities, they feel that
the stupidities are actually larger than they really are.

Just kick back and relax folks. If you don't like a message, don't
always reply -- the delete key in your mail reader is there for a
reason. Always remind yourself "this is just a bunch of writing going
by -- its not to be taken more seriously than that". If all else
fails, take a vacation from the list for 48 hours. You'd be suprised
how much less important things seem if you ignore them for a couple of
days.

Talk to y'all later.

Perry

