Date: Tue, 12 Jan 93 12:06:07 -0800
From: David Jevans <jevans@apple.com>
Subject: EVOL New Genes

I wonder what new genes will come into effect in human evolution given
the vastly different environment that we live in.  There are likely numerous
genes for intellectualism, understanding of complex mathematics and large
systems, dynamism, etc. that will determine who migrates to the trans-human
stage.  These could be long dormant genes that have been irrelevant to
previous evolution.

>Embryological development of our genetic code can hold simple rules,
>such as the "universal grammar" from which all human languages are
>constructed, but it can't hold  anything so ambitious as the "secrets
>of the universe".  Even if it did, or held something less ambitious
>like a tuner for galactic alien Channel 3, mutation & selection would
>have quickly wiped it out and replaced it with something more
>conducive to the task of propagating genes in hunter-gatherer tribes.

Such genes could be very small, thereby having greater chance of surviving
long periods unaltered.  Similarly, they could have been entirely
invisible from the selection process until the modern environment.

More interestingly, McKenna and others have posited that the agent of
human evolution has been the shaman.  One who has transcended learned
culture, language, and thinking to see beyond the current situation and
methodologies and envisioned the future.  BY bringing back that vision
in the form of art, storytelling, tribal leadership, and, more recently,
technology, the shaman has been the driving force behind leaps of
'social evolution'.  In "Creative Brainstorms (subtitle something about
Madness and Creativity)", there are a number of studies of men who have
(oops, and women) who have shaped humanity such as van Gough, DaVinci, etc.
These people often exhibited psychotic kinds of behaviours during their
most creative periods.  Episodes similar to those that occur under the
influence of psychedelics.  In fact, many of these people had chemical
dependencies to absynth and the like.  There is an interseting EKG plot
of brainwaves in normal states and under the influence of LSD and
mescaline.

Anyway, the key idea here is that perhaps the gene for psychedelic affinity
is a survival gene, as it has been influential in the development of
human culture.

-dj

