Date: Tue, 12 Jan 1993 14:34:55 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Re: EVOL New Genes

David Jenkins:
> There are likely numerous
> genes for intellectualism, understanding of complex mathematics and large
> systems, dynamism, etc. that will determine who migrates to the trans-human
> stage.

If so, such genes are in decline.  Cross-culturally since the 19th
century in the developed world, and mid-20th century in the 3rd world, 
fertility has been strongly, negatively correlated with education level.
This is likely caused by (a) differential access to and ability to use 
birth control, and (b) the child-raising vs. career tradeoff.  
Correlations with other factors are less clear; for example wealth
often gives a U-curve, with the middle class having the lowest fertility.

There are many ways around this.  We've already discussed smart drugs
and neural transplants.  Intelligence-lowering syndromes with chemical 
causes (eg most depression and schizophrenia) often respond to chemicals.
The state of creativity you discuss is characteristic of mania;
manic-depressives respond to lithium and it is possible that a mild,
creative mania can be chemically induced.  Furthermore, we might be able
to find "hi-IQ" genes and introduce them, either through somatic or
germ-line therapy, or by transplating fetal neurons cloned from
geniuses.  Migration to the transhuman stage will almost entirely be 
a process of engineering, not evolution.

A related problem not solved by these methods is the 
self-genociding space colony.  This effect could decrease 
cultural diversity, slow growth to the transhuman stage, and 
stall space colonization, halting the transhuman migration to 
the stars.  To solve this, transhumans will need pro-natal memes 
and related technology, eg to reverse menopause and fix the 
genetic errors common to older parent births. 

