David Jevans writes:
>>Isn't quantum mechanics "proof" that we are living in a virtual reality
>>with finite precision.

>No.  First, quantum mechanics is no more easily modeled by a finite
>celluar autonoma than non-QM physics.

I'm not sure I understand the relevance of cellular autonoma.  Could you
elucidate?  My thoughts were along the line that when we try to represent
data in a computer with fixed resolution numbers (ie. any computer with
fixed amount of memory) we run into precision problems and resort to
statistical methods, interval mathematics, or live with inaccuracies.
I see a parallel with quantum physics in that you can view it as there
not being enough resolution in the fabric of the universe (whatever that
means) to exactly represent in space and time where an electron is going
to be.  So we resort to probabilistic techniques.

> Second, as long as the idea that the universe really is following QM is
> consistent with what we see, it can't be excluded in preference to the
> hypothesis that we're all being simulated.

Well yeah.  That's the classical epistomological problem.  How can you
prove you're not a brain in a vat, hooked up to someone's VR machine.
You can't.  You can only live in the universe presented to you, and
have fun thinking about possibilities of others.

