Date: Tue, 12 Jan 93 17:40:50 -0800
From: David Jevans <jevans@apple.com>
Subject: Re: Avoiding death

>Back to donorcycles--could some of the many motorcycle-riding folks
>explain why they take such enormous risks while also fretting about
>the prospects for immortality?

We're not old enough to realize that we're mortal?

Actually, we're addicted to adrenaline, experience, power, concentration, etc.
Riding a racebike quickly is the finest balance of mind over matter that
I have ever experienced.  At speeds greater than 120mph with your tender
knee dragging on the ground, controlling a bike within centimeters of a
visualized 'line' through a corner, requires incredible concentration.

To achieve this, you cannot think of anything else.  Not even for 1/4
of a second.  If you ever even look at the ground you get nauseous and
disoriented.  You've got to look as far ahead as you can.  When scraping
through a corner you are already looking through it to the next one.
You steer through the current corner purely on instinct, peripheral vision,
and memory of what you saw several seconds prior.

At these levels of concentration and future visualization, our conciousness
seems to change so that the ego leaves the body, and floats in front of
the bike.  In fact, the bike disappears and it actually becomes you and
you it.  Seriously.  You are completely obsessed with processing visual
data without translating it into language.  This is a very direct form
of visual thinking.  When you are actually racing you then reintroduce
a bit of your "normal" ego-conciousness in order to think about strategy
and tactics. These processes go on in parallel.  When you let your
concentration slip or let the ego get hold, you crash.  :-(

It usually takes me about 1 hour of intense riding to achieve this kind
of truly altered state.  To "get into it" so to speak.  If I haven't been
riding for some days or weeks than it takes perhaps days to regain it.

Other reasons are that you really get a feeling for the terrain.  You
commune with nature, as absurd as it may seem hurlting along at high
speed on a 400 pound machine.  The wind, smells, temperature changes, changes
in elevation, road camber, etc are all immediately, viscerally apparent.
You lean into corners, not out of them as you do in a car.  You steer with
your whole body, instinctively, rather than going through a more artificially
derived interface such as the steering wheel.  You never have to move your
hands or feet to shift. Throttle and brakes are in your hands, which are way
way way more sensitive than feet.  A good rider can control the front brake
so well that he can stand the bike literally on its nose, the rear tire 2
feet or more in the air, the front tire on the verge of locking up, at 100mph!

Why ignore the danger?  Firstly, we all believe that we are better than 
average and a wreck won't happen to us (yep, we're deluded).  But primarily,
it is an addiction.  During those altered states of such intense concentration
and adrenaline rush, it is better than sex.  It is more fulfilling than drugs,
and you can get better at it!  It is a direct connection from idea to action.

When you see some fellow all decked out in colorful leathers on a 130hp
race machine riding along the road, he is not in the same world as you
in a car.  He is out there risking it all, reveling in the exactitude
and perfection of executing what is visualized.  A warrior, a hero
in his own mind.  In CA all riders wave to each other as they
pass.  This is a special community of people who know and accept the risks
We live in a world where we see more danger, make more heroic saves from the
jaws of death every day than most people will in a lifetime. (Or so it
seems during the adrenaline high).

Get it?  :-)

-dj


